♪ (Teammate) No need to worry! Just leave it to me! ♪

12
00:01:06.757 --> 00:01:13.607
♪ I'll save us a fortnight— We'll ship it by three! ♪

13
00:01:13.607 --> 00:01:15.767
[music]

14
00:01:15.767 --> 00:01:16.938
(narrator) Act one, Requirements.

15
00:01:16.938 --> 00:01:22.424
(narrator) A long scroll of requirements unrolls behind the Teammate, and he snaps it shut with one raised finger.

16
00:01:22.424 --> 00:01:28.549
(narrator) A giant phone running the coffee app, CoffeeGo, shows a cup to go, then one big order button and a latte, gleaming.

17
00:01:28.549 --> 00:01:33.716
(narrator) Silhouetted hands in the audience go up with question marks; he shushes them and taps his temple.

18
00:01:33.716 --> 00:01:39.681
(narrator) A card for a meeting with users, What do they need?, appears, and he tears it in two and tosses the halves away.

19
00:01:39.681 --> 00:01:43.782
(narrator) He bows to a lit Applause sign, and the house stays silent but for a cricket.

20
00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:47.360
♪ Requirements? Quite simple! It's coffee to go! ♪

21
00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:50.820
♪ No need to ask users— I'm certain I know! ♪

22
00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:54.220
♪ One button! One latte! How sleek! How refined! ♪

23
00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:58.700
♪ I'll save them a meeting— They'll thank me in time! ♪

24
00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:01.033
[music]

25
00:02:01.033 --> 00:02:12.186
(narrator) The Product Manager glides in from the right, with your hero's face, a navy blazer over a coral tee, a lanyard and badge, grey trousers, white sneakers, and a clipboard, and stands with hands on hips.

26
00:02:12.186 --> 00:02:16.536
(narrator) The Teammate freezes with a sheepish grin and a bead of sweat while they sing.

27
00:02:16.536 --> 00:02:22.112
(narrator) The phone steps back behind a long counter, where twenty empty cups line up; the clock turns to ten.

28
00:02:22.112 --> 00:02:25.625
(narrator) One cup slides out of the phone, and twenty straws fan into it.

29
00:02:25.625 --> 00:02:30.030
(narrator) The Product Manager shows a card: Demo today, group order, 20 lattes for 10:00.

30
00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:38.730
(narrator) Then the phone fans out into six small phones, red, orange, gold, green, blue, and violet, each with the same one button, and the Product Manager facepalms.

31
00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:43.000
♪ (Product Manager) They need twenty coffees For meetings at ten! ♪

32
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:46.420
♪ Your app sends one NOW— Shall they share it, then? ♪

33
00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:49.980
♪ I promised a demo Of what they could use. ♪

34
00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:54.167
♪ You've built the wrong product— In six lovely hues! ♪

35
00:02:54.467 --> 00:02:55.350
(narrator) Act two, Reuse.

36
00:02:55.350 --> 00:03:02.412
(narrator) A wall shelf of package boxes slides in: auth, charts, orders, emoji, dates, and payments, each with its version number.

37
00:03:02.412 --> 00:03:06.473
(narrator) A sticker says Reuse!, and a red card says Build it anew: no, thanks.

38
00:03:06.473 --> 00:03:11.476
(narrator) The orders package flies off the shelf into the phone, which now sends a group order.

39
00:03:11.476 --> 00:03:21.188
(narrator) He flicks away a padlocked document, package-lock.json, and new versions of the orders package march in from the right on little legs, each getting a sticker: fixes!

40
00:03:21.188 --> 00:03:23.365
(narrator) He holds a gold trophy high, beaming.

41
00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:27.640
♪ (Teammate) Group orders? Quite simple! I'll borrow the code! ♪

42
00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:30.740
♪ Design with reuse! Why build it anew? ♪

43
00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:34.220
♪ A package for orders Will know what to do! ♪

44
00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:37.700
♪ No lockfile to fuss with! Let new versions in! ♪

45
00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:45.067
♪ New versions mean fixes— A sensible win! ♪

46
00:03:45.367 --> 00:03:53.571
(narrator) The Build Engineer glides in from the right, with your hero's face, a hi-vis safety vest over a navy tee, olive cargo trousers, work boots, and a wrench.

47
00:03:53.571 --> 00:03:55.555
(narrator) The Teammate freezes again, sweating.

48
00:03:55.555 --> 00:04:01.560
(narrator) His laptop keeps the old orders package, while a blue build machine marked Clean build takes in the new version.

49
00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:06.762
(narrator) A plug for the old call bounces off a socket for the new interface, with a spark and a red cross.

50
00:04:06.762 --> 00:04:13.250
(narrator) The toy ship is stamped Can't ship, every lamp on the Build status board turns red, and a sticker says All builds failed.

51
00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:20.489
(narrator) Last, his laptop drops into a cardboard box marked Overnight, a label slaps on, Fragile: works on my machine, and he clutches his head.

52
00:04:21.083 --> 00:04:25.023
♪ (Build Engineer) Your laptop kept old ones; My clean build got new. ♪

53
00:04:25.023 --> 00:04:27.953
♪ The interface changed— Your calls won't go through! ♪

54
00:04:27.953 --> 00:04:31.593
♪ We can't ship the software; The fresh builds all fail. ♪

55
00:04:31.593 --> 00:04:36.393
♪ Shall I ship them your laptop By overnight mail? ♪

56
00:04:36.393 --> 00:04:38.200
[music]

57
00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:38.652
(narrator) Refrain.

58
00:04:38.652 --> 00:04:42.662
(narrator) The Teammate stands by his desk, where his laptop shows All tests pass.

59
00:04:42.662 --> 00:04:48.142
(narrator) He lifts it high on one palm in a golden light while the Build status board behind him stays red.

60
00:04:48.142 --> 00:04:52.774
(narrator) Back on the desk, it shows his code in a gilded frame, and he blows a chef's kiss.

61
00:04:52.774 --> 00:04:56.445
(narrator) A flip counter on the desk, Tiny fixes, counts 1, then 5, then 6.

62
00:04:56.445 --> 00:05:03.845
(narrator) Last, six lamps on his laptop's screen turn green, the footlights turn green, and he throws his arms up, while the board stays red.

63
00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:07.900
♪ (Teammate) It works on my machine! ♪

64
00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:11.280
♪ The finest code you've seen! ♪

65
00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:14.600
♪ One tiny fix— Or five or six— ♪

66
00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:17.967
♪ And all the lights turn green! ♪

67
00:05:18.267 --> 00:05:19.429
(narrator) Act three, Testing.

68
00:05:19.429 --> 00:05:26.284
(narrator) A code card, checkout.py, holds a function that loads a cart, takes a subtotal, adds tax, and returns the total.

69
00:05:26.284 --> 00:05:31.058
(narrator) Gold test tags pin onto every call, and green numbers 1 to 4 onto their order.

70
00:05:31.058 --> 00:05:36.137
(narrator) A second card, test_checkout.py, asserts that the calls come in exactly that order.

71
00:05:36.137 --> 00:05:39.259
(narrator) A plaster mould closes round the code, sealed Cast.

72
00:05:39.259 --> 00:05:42.808
(narrator) The add-tax line flashes red, and a big red stamp says NO!

73
00:05:42.808 --> 00:05:43.543
(narrator) on the test.

74
00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:47.680
♪ I'll make this more solid! I'll test every call! ♪

75
00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:51.000
♪ The order of helpers— I'll check it all! ♪

76
00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:54.060
♪ My implementation Is captured just so! ♪

77
00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:57.533
♪ If one small step changes, The tests will say "NO!" ♪

78
00:05:57.833 --> 00:06:06.922
(narrator) The Test Engineer glides in from the right, with your hero's face, a white lab coat over a lilac shirt, pens in the pocket, goggles round the neck, and a magnifying glass.

79
00:06:06.922 --> 00:06:08.570
(narrator) The Teammate freezes, sweating.

80
00:06:08.570 --> 00:06:15.373
(narrator) In the code, the subtotal line now takes tax, the add-tax line is struck out, and a last line says checkout is still 42 dollars.

81
00:06:15.373 --> 00:06:21.167
(narrator) But the test card cracks and turns red, stamped Failed; night falls in the window, and the clock runs to two.

82
00:06:21.167 --> 00:06:31.212
(narrator) Four little test cards with faces cling to the bottom of the code and shriek, a sticker asks Refactor?, hearts float up, and the Test Engineer tugs at the code while the tests haul it back.

83
00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:35.930
♪ (Test Engineer) I moved one small helper; The results stayed right. ♪

84
00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:39.400
♪ Your brittle tests failed— There goes my whole night! ♪

85
00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:43.390
♪ They test implementation! A refactor? Oh, no! ♪

86
00:06:43.390 --> 00:06:47.690
♪ They're in love with your old code And won't let it go! ♪

87
00:06:48.267 --> 00:06:49.827
(narrator) Act four, Information hiding.

88
00:06:49.827 --> 00:06:54.185
(narrator) The Orders module is a little building with two doors, which he shoves wide open.

89
00:06:54.185 --> 00:07:00.479
(narrator) Inside is its table, orders, with an item and a date on each row, wrapped in two layers marked API and Service layer.

90
00:07:00.479 --> 00:07:07.904
(narrator) Three other modules stand at the right as little houses, Checkout, Billing, and Mail, and each reaches a long arm straight into the table.

91
00:07:07.904 --> 00:07:13.983
(narrator) He tears both wrappers away, gives a thumbs-up, and changes the date format, from month, day, year to year first.

92
00:07:14.593 --> 00:07:17.753
♪ (Teammate) Information hiding? Why shut any doors? ♪

93
00:07:17.753 --> 00:07:21.153
♪ My tables are open— What's mine can be yours! ♪

94
00:07:21.153 --> 00:07:24.173
♪ No wrappers! No layers! Just read them direct! ♪

95
00:07:24.173 --> 00:07:28.167
♪ I'll change one date format— What could that affect? ♪

96
00:07:28.467 --> 00:07:39.465
(narrator) The UI Developer glides in from the right, with your hero's face, a mustard turtleneck under a denim painter's apron with dabs of paint and a brush in its pocket, rust trousers, and a palette.

97
00:07:39.465 --> 00:07:41.241
(narrator) The Teammate freezes, sweating.

98
00:07:41.241 --> 00:07:44.507
(narrator) Checkout, Billing, and Mail crack, each with a red cross.

99
00:07:44.507 --> 00:07:52.813
(narrator) The dates glow red, and dashed red lines race out to eight chips: receipts, reports, exports, labels, invoices, search, reminders, and analytics.

100
00:07:52.813 --> 00:08:01.520
(narrator) Bandages go on, a counter of open bugs climbs to 42, and as day and night race past the window, the UI Developer's hair turns silver and a cane appears.

101
00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:05.890
♪ (UI Developer) Now checkout and billing And mail need repair! ♪

102
00:08:05.890 --> 00:08:09.400
♪ One detail has changed— It's used EVERYWHERE! ♪

103
00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:12.760
♪ The tests fight each patch; There's no end in sight! ♪

104
00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:18.050
♪ You changed one date format— I've aged overnight! ♪

105
00:08:18.050 --> 00:08:19.633
[music]

106
00:08:19.633 --> 00:08:20.052
(narrator) Refrain.

107
00:08:20.052 --> 00:08:29.673
(narrator) Again the Teammate lifts his laptop high on one palm in a golden light while the Build status board stays red, sets it back to show his code in a gilded frame, and blows a chef's kiss.

108
00:08:29.673 --> 00:08:32.340
(narrator) This time the Tiny fixes counter goes from 1 to 26.

109
00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:35.112
(narrator) His laptop's six lamps and the footlights turn green.

110
00:08:35.710 --> 00:08:39.130
♪ (Teammate) It works on my machine! ♪

111
00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:42.400
♪ The finest code you've seen! ♪

112
00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:45.830
♪ One tiny fix— Or twenty-six— ♪

113
00:08:45.830 --> 00:08:48.967
♪ And all the lights turn green! ♪

114
00:08:49.267 --> 00:08:50.736
(narrator) Act five, Shared history.

115
00:08:50.736 --> 00:08:56.612
(narrator) A commit graph shows shared main: an old base commit and four teammates' commits, each labelled fix.

116
00:08:56.612 --> 00:09:01.842
(narrator) Below it runs his wiggly branch of six small commits: wip, oops, fix, typo, wip2, and eh.

117
00:09:01.842 --> 00:09:03.664
(narrator) They fold into one tidy commit.

118
00:09:03.664 --> 00:09:09.540
(narrator) He force-pushes it onto main, knocking the four fix commits off the line, under a stamp: Force push.

119
00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:16.356
(narrator) He sweeps the fallen commits away with a broom, and a card says Code review: 1 commit to read, with a sticker, Easy!

120
00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:20.240
♪ I'll squash all my changes To one tidy line! ♪

121
00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:23.980
♪ Then force-push to shared main— That history will shine! ♪

122
00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:26.960
♪ One clean line of progress! Less clutter to see! ♪

123
00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:31.167
♪ Review will be easy— That's teamwork from me! ♪

124
00:09:31.467 --> 00:09:40.967
(narrator) The Back-End Developer glides in from the right, with your hero's face, a charcoal hoodie with a little database on the chest, black jeans, sneakers, and a coffee mug.

125
00:09:40.967 --> 00:09:42.731
(narrator) The Teammate freezes, sweating.

126
00:09:42.731 --> 00:09:45.632
(narrator) Dashed outlines show where their four commits were.

127
00:09:45.632 --> 00:09:51.663
(narrator) The old base commit gets cobwebs and a tag, old; every lamp on the board turns red, under a stamp: Broken.

128
00:09:51.663 --> 00:09:53.711
(narrator) A card says My clone: 4 fixes, safe.

129
00:09:53.711 --> 00:09:59.912
(narrator) The fix commits drop back onto main after his, dawn breaks, the clock turns to six, and the lamps turn green.

130
00:09:59.912 --> 00:10:05.999
(narrator) Two calendar cards, Saturday and Sunday, both free, drift up and vanish, and the Back-End Developer slumps.

131
00:10:06.593 --> 00:10:10.593
♪ (Back-End Developer) I pushed all our fixes! They're gone from shared main! ♪

132
00:10:10.593 --> 00:10:14.173
♪ You forced your OLD history— We're broken again! ♪

133
00:10:14.173 --> 00:10:17.863
♪ My commits are safe here; I'll restore them by dawn. ♪

134
00:10:17.863 --> 00:10:22.533
♪ Your graph is much cleaner— My weekend is gone! ♪

135
00:10:22.533 --> 00:10:24.733
[music]

136
00:10:24.733 --> 00:10:25.681
(narrator) Act six, Secrets.

137
00:10:25.681 --> 00:10:34.818
(narrator) He pats his chest and tucks the Back-End Developer into a purple checked blanket; they yawn and doze off on their feet, with Z's drifting up, then slide off asleep.

138
00:10:34.818 --> 00:10:41.281
(narrator) A dark card shows the key in .env, and in app.py the hard-coded key is struck out for one read from the environment.

139
00:10:41.281 --> 00:10:43.232
(narrator) A sticker says Safer!, and he bows.

140
00:10:43.232 --> 00:10:54.654
(narrator) Then a folder, my-app, holds app.py, a readme, and .env with a key; with git add, all three fly into a commit box, and with git push the box flies up into a cloud marked Public repo, where three keys hang.

141
00:10:54.654 --> 00:10:59.334
(narrator) Gold keys rain down from the cloud into the audience, whose hands reach up for them.

142
00:10:59.947 --> 00:11:03.467
♪ (Teammate) I'll take the deployment! You get some sleep! ♪

143
00:11:03.467 --> 00:11:06.507
♪ The keys live in dot-env, Not hard-coded now! ♪

144
00:11:06.507 --> 00:11:09.987
♪ A safer deployment— Allow me to bow! ♪

145
00:11:09.987 --> 00:11:13.587
♪ I add the whole folder; One push, and we're done! ♪

146
00:11:13.587 --> 00:11:21.267
♪ At last, a smooth handoff For everyone! ♪

147
00:11:21.567 --> 00:11:33.238
(narrator) The Security Engineer glides in from the right, with your hero's face, a black tactical jacket with chest pockets, epaulettes, a gold shield badge, and an earpiece wire, black cargo trousers, and boots.

148
00:11:33.238 --> 00:11:35.029
(narrator) The Teammate freezes, sweating.

149
00:11:35.029 --> 00:11:40.344
(narrator) A card shows .gitignore without .env, and a red card shows Commit 4f2a, with the keys in it.

150
00:11:40.344 --> 00:11:43.175
(narrator) Two masked burglars grab the keys from the cloud.

151
00:11:43.175 --> 00:11:49.473
(narrator) Three server racks spin up, the board turns red, and a cloud bill climbs to minus 48,200 dollars, in the red.

152
00:11:49.473 --> 00:11:52.420
(narrator) A rocket marked Theirs, on bags of money, launches.

153
00:11:52.420 --> 00:11:58.775
(narrator) The Teammate adds .env to .gitignore, but the commit is still there, and five copies, each with a key, pop up.

154
00:11:58.775 --> 00:12:02.646
(narrator) The Security Engineer stamps them Revoked as the bill climbs again.

155
00:12:03.247 --> 00:12:06.427
♪ (Security Engineer) Dot-env's not in gitignore— You committed the keys! ♪

156
00:12:06.427 --> 00:12:09.777
♪ Our PUBLIC repo hands them To strangers with ease! ♪

157
00:12:09.777 --> 00:12:13.817
♪ Thieves are spinning up servers; We're deep in the red! ♪

158
00:12:13.817 --> 00:12:17.967
♪ You wanted a launch— We're funding THEIRS instead! ♪

159
00:12:18.377 --> 00:12:19.987
♪ (Teammate) I'll add it to gitignore! ♪

160
00:12:19.987 --> 00:12:23.377
♪ (Security Engineer) That won't uncommit it! Old copies remain! ♪

161
00:12:23.377 --> 00:12:28.033
♪ Revoke those credentials— They're spending again! ♪

162
00:12:28.333 --> 00:12:28.722
(narrator) Finale.

163
00:12:28.722 --> 00:12:34.947
(narrator) All six teammates come back as a chorus line behind him, hands on hips; the UI Developer is still silver-haired.

164
00:12:34.947 --> 00:12:39.282
(narrator) He lifts his laptop high on one palm once more, then sets it back on the desk.

165
00:12:39.282 --> 00:12:44.451
(narrator) The Tiny fixes counter jumps from 1 to 96, and the six teammates facepalm, one after another.

166
00:12:44.451 --> 00:12:53.232
(narrator) His laptop's lamps and the footlights turn green, confetti bursts, and he holds the last note, arms high, while the teammates despair and the board stays red.

167
00:12:53.827 --> 00:12:57.627
♪ (Teammate) It works on my machine! ♪

168
00:12:57.627 --> 00:13:00.947
♪ The finest code you've seen! ♪

169
00:13:00.947 --> 00:13:03.657
♪ One tiny fix— Or ninety-six— ♪

170
00:13:03.657 --> 00:13:10.867
♪ And all the lights turn green! ♪

171
00:13:11.167 --> 00:13:11.934
(narrator) Outro, spoken.

172
00:13:11.934 --> 00:13:12.810
(narrator) The music stops.

173
00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:17.467
(narrator) His laptop now shows a red cross and the words Fatal error; he frowns at it, puzzled.

174
00:13:17.467 --> 00:13:23.878
(narrator) He steps to the desk and reaches for the keyboard with a sly smile, and the Security Engineer slips off to the right.

175
00:13:23.878 --> 00:13:31.767
(narrator) Then the Security Engineer glides in from the left, between him and the desk, palm out; he jumps back with both hands up, and the teammates nod.

176
00:13:31.767 --> 00:13:38.013
(narrator) On the closing chord the curtain falls, and the moral of the opera is posted on it: Ask your users what they need.

177
00:13:38.013 --> 00:13:39.273
(narrator) Lock your dependencies.

178
00:13:39.273 --> 00:13:41.191
(narrator) Test behaviour, not implementation.

179
00:13:41.191 --> 00:13:42.889
(narrator) Hide details behind interfaces.

180
00:13:42.889 --> 00:13:44.697
(narrator) Never force-push a shared branch.

181
00:13:44.697 --> 00:13:46.012
(narrator) Keep secrets out of Git.

182
00:13:46.603 --> 00:13:50.223
Strange. It worked on my machine.

183
00:13:50.583 --> 00:13:53.013
I'll just make one small change.

184
00:13:53.203 --> 00:13:56.533
(Security Engineer) Step away from the keyboard.

185
00:13:56.533 --> 00:14:03.467
[music]
